---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import Nav from "@/components/nav.astro";
---

<Layout title="NFKC Normalization: Where Sanitization Fails">
  <Nav />
  <main class="max-w-3xl mx-auto pt-28 md:pt-32 pb-16 text-[var(--white)]">
    <a
      href="/blogs/"
      class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] hover:text-white transition-colors mb-8"
    >
      <svg
        xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
        fill="currentColor"
        class="size-4"
      >
        <path
          d="M7.82843 10.9999H20V12.9999H7.82843L13.1924 18.3638L11.7782 19.778L4 11.9999L11.7782 4.22168L13.1924 5.63589L7.82843 10.9999Z"
        ></path>
      </svg>
      Back to blogs
    </a>

    <header class="mb-10">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 mb-3 text-sm">
        <span
          class="px-3 py-1 rounded-full border border-1 border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] bg-[#1414149c] text-[var(--sec)]"
        >
          Unicode
        </span>
        <span
          class="px-3 py-1 rounded-full border border-1 border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] bg-[#1414149c] text-[var(--white-icon)]"
        >
          Python
        </span>
        <span
          class="px-3 py-1 rounded-full border border-1 border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] bg-[#1414149c] text-[var(--white-icon)]"
        >
          Web Security
        </span>
        <span class="text-[var(--white-icon)]">Nov 11, 2026</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-semibold mb-3">
        NFKC Normalization: Where Sanitization Fails
      </h1>
      <p class="text-[var(--white-icon)]">
        Two strings can look identical, compare differently, and still end up as
        the same value after normalization. That gap is where the interesting
        bugs live.
      </p>

      <div
        class="transform"
        aria-label="The fullwidth letter A, U+FF21, normalizes to the ASCII letter A, U+0041"
      >
        <div class="transform__side">
          <span class="transform__char">Ａ</span>
          <span class="transform__meta">U+FF21 · fullwidth</span>
        </div>
        <div class="transform__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
        <div class="transform__side transform__side--out">
          <span class="transform__char transform__char--accent">A</span>
          <span class="transform__meta">U+0041 · ASCII</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <article class="writeup space-y-5">
      <div class="callout">
        <span class="callout__label">Nutshell</span>
        <p>
          NFKC folds <strong>compatibility characters</strong> into their
          standard form, fullwidth Ａ becomes A, ① becomes 1, and ／ becomes /.
          If one component validates the raw input while another processes the
          normalized input, the check and the action can disagree.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Unicode in 60 seconds</h2>
      <p>
        Unicode assigns every character a number called a <strong>code point</strong>.
        Most of the time this is invisible, until two strings look the same but
        are not the same:
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Character</th>
              <th>Code point</th>
              <th>Name</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><code>A</code></td>
              <td><code>U+0041</code></td>
              <td>Latin capital letter A</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>a</code></td>
              <td><code>U+0061</code></td>
              <td>Latin small letter a</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>①</code></td>
              <td><code>U+2460</code></td>
              <td>Circled digit one</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>²</code></td>
              <td><code>U+00B2</code></td>
              <td>Superscript two</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>Ａ</code></td>
              <td><code>U+FF21</code></td>
              <td>Fullwidth Latin capital letter A</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        <code>A</code> (<span class="cp">U+0041</span>) and <code>Ａ</code>
        (<span class="cp">U+FF21</span>) look almost the same, but they are
        different code points. To a string comparison, they are different
        characters even though a human would call them the same letter.
      </p>

      <h2>Normalization: same character, different bytes</h2>
      <p>
        Unicode normalization turns equivalent representations into a
        consistent one. The classic example is <code>é</code>, which can be
        stored in two ways:
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Input</th>
              <th>Code points</th>
              <th>After NFC</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><code>é</code> (precomposed)</td>
              <td><code>U+00E9</code></td>
              <td><code>U+00E9</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>e</code> + combining acute</td>
              <td><code>U+0065 U+0301</code></td>
              <td><code>U+00E9</code></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Both inputs display as the same character, and both normalize to the
        same code point. This is <strong>canonical equivalence</strong>: a
        difference in encoding, not in meaning.
      </p>

      <h2>NFKC: compatibility counts too</h2>
      <p>
        NFKC stands for <strong>Normalization Form Compatibility Composition</strong>.
        On top of canonical equivalence, it also applies <strong>compatibility
        mappings</strong> formatting variants, ligatures, circled characters,
        superscripts, fullwidth forms.
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Input</th>
              <th>Code point</th>
              <th>NFKC result</th>
              <th>Why</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><code>Ａ</code></td>
              <td><code>U+FF21</code></td>
              <td><code>A</code></td>
              <td>Fullwidth form</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>①</code></td>
              <td><code>U+2460</code></td>
              <td><code>1</code></td>
              <td>Circled digit</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>²</code></td>
              <td><code>U+00B2</code></td>
              <td><code>2</code></td>
              <td>Superscript</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>ﬁ</code></td>
              <td><code>U+FB01</code></td>
              <td><code>fi</code></td>
              <td>Ligature</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>／</code></td>
              <td><code>U+FF0F</code></td>
              <td><code>/</code></td>
              <td>Fullwidth solidus</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Python ships the whole normalization family in <code>unicodedata</code>:</p>

      <pre><code>import unicodedata

payload = "Ａ①²ﬁ"
normalized = unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", payload)

print(payload)     # Ａ①²ﬁ
print(normalized)  # A12fi</code></pre>

      <p>
        NFC and NFD preserve compatibility distinctions. NFKC and NFKD do not,
        they deliberately collapse them. That is the feature. It is also the
        risk.
      </p>

      <h2>Where it bites</h2>
      <p>
        NFKC is not a vulnerability by itself. The bug appears when two parts of
        a system disagree about <em>which form is canonical</em>: one validates
        the input as received, the other processes it after normalization.
      </p>

      <h3>1. Validation bypass</h3>
      <p>
        Imagine a validator that blocks forward slashes to keep user-controlled
        paths in check:
      </p>

      <pre><code>def validate_input(value):
    return "/" not in value</code></pre>

      <p>
        The fullwidth solidus <code>／</code>
        (<span class="cp">U+FF0F</span>) is not a forward slash, so it passes.
        Then the value is normalized:
      </p>

      <pre><code>payload = "／etc/passwd"

print(validate_input(payload))                      # True
print(unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", payload))       # /etc/passwd</code></pre>

      <div class="callout callout--note">
        <span class="callout__label">Important</span>
        <p>
          This is a primitive, not a complete path traversal. It matters when
          the normalized value reaches a sink, a filesystem call, a query, a
          shell, a template, or an access-control decision.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>The dangerous pattern looks like this:</p>

      <pre><code>if validate_input(user_input):
    process(unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", user_input))</code></pre>

      <p>
        The validator inspected one string. The rest of the application acted on
        another.
      </p>

      <h3>2. WAF sees one thing, the backend sees another</h3>
      <p>
        The same mismatch scales up to a Web Application Firewall. The WAF
        inspects the request as it arrived; the backend may normalize later.
      </p>

      <div class="flow">
        <div class="flow__step">
          <span>01 · Request</span>
          <strong>Attacker sends</strong>
          <code>／etc/passwd</code>
          <small class="flow__meta">U+FF0F before “etc/passwd”</small>
        </div>
        <div class="flow__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
        <div class="flow__step">
          <span>02 · Inspect</span>
          <strong>WAF sees</strong>
          <code>／</code>
          <small class="flow__meta">not ASCII “/”</small>
        </div>
        <div class="flow__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
        <div class="flow__step flow__step--accent">
          <span>03 · Process</span>
          <strong>Backend sees</strong>
          <code>/etc/passwd</code>
          <small class="flow__meta">after NFKC</small>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        This does not mean "NFKC bypasses WAFs." It depends on how the WAF
        handles Unicode, how the framework normalizes, and the order in which
        each layer runs. The testable question is whether those layers agree.
      </p>

      <h3>3. Username collisions</h3>
      <p>
        Now consider registration and login. These two usernames are different
        strings:
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Username</th>
              <th>Code points</th>
              <th>NFKC</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><code>admin</code></td>
              <td><code>U+0061 U+0064 U+006D U+0069 U+006E</code></td>
              <td><code>admin</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>ａｄｍｉｎ</code></td>
              <td><code>U+FF41 U+FF44 U+FF4D U+FF49 U+FF4E</code></td>
              <td><code>admin</code></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        If the uniqueness check looks at the raw value but authentication looks
        up the normalized value or vice versa, two different accounts can map
        to the same identifier. Depending on the order of the checks, that is
        either a collision, a broken login, or a very bad day.
      </p>

      <p>
        Python makes this especially visible because the parser applies NFKC to
        identifiers (PEP 3131). Fullwidth names are not separate names:
      </p>

      <pre><code>ａｄｍｉｎ = 42
print(admin)  # 42</code></pre>

      <h2>How to defend</h2>
      <p>
        Pick one normalization policy and apply it at the boundary, before any
        validation or uniqueness check. Then keep using that same value.
      </p>

      <pre><code>import unicodedata

def normalize_username(value: str) -&gt; str:
    value = unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", value)
    if not value.isascii() or not value.isalnum():
        raise ValueError("invalid username")
    return value.lower()</code></pre>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Normalize first, validate second.</strong> Validating the raw input creates the gap.</li>
        <li><strong>Store the normalized form.</strong> Apply the same transform to uniqueness checks, login lookups, and authorization decisions.</li>
        <li><strong>Decide per field.</strong> A username policy is not automatically a password policy. Normalizing arbitrary content can change its meaning.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep other layers aligned.</strong> Database collation, case folding, and confusable-character checks are separate decisions, not substitutes for normalization.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Takeaway</h2>
      <p>
        Unicode normalization is a consistency problem before it is a security
        problem. NFKC is simply the form that makes the gap easiest to reach:
        it turns lookalike input into ordinary ASCII.
      </p>

      <p>
        So the next time an application validates one string and processes
        another, try this:
      </p>

      <pre><code>import unicodedata

for char in "ｐａｙｌｏａｄ／①":
    normalized = unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", char)
    if char != normalized:
        print(f"U+&#123;ord(char):04X&#125; &#123;char!r&#125; -&gt; &#123;normalized!r&#125;")</code></pre>

      <p>
        If the filter sees something different from the sink, you have found the
        seam. That is where the bug is.
      </p>
    </article>

    <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-[#ffffff10] flex justify-between items-center">
      <a
        href="/blogs/"
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] hover:text-white transition-colors"
      >
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor" class="size-4">
          <path
            d="M7.82843 10.9999H20V12.9999H7.82843L13.1924 18.3638L11.7782 19.778L4 11.9999L11.7782 4.22168L13.1924 5.63589L7.82843 10.9999Z"
          ></path>
        </svg>
        Back to blogs
      </a>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .writeup {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: var(--white);
  }

  .writeup h2 {
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--white);
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .writeup h3 {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--white);
    padding-top: 0.75rem;
  }

  .writeup strong {
    color: #fff;
  }

  .writeup em {
    color: var(--white-icon);
  }

  .writeup code {
    background: #1414149c;
    border: 1px solid var(--white-icon-tr);
    border-radius: 0.4rem;
    padding: 0.08rem 0.3rem;
    font-size: 0.85em;
    color: var(--sec);
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    word-break: break-all;
  }

  .writeup .cp {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 0.82em;
    color: var(--sec);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .writeup pre {
    background: #1414149c;
    border: 1px solid var(--white-icon-tr);
    border-radius: 1rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .writeup pre code {
    background: none;
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
    color: #dfdfdf;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    word-break: normal;
  }

  .writeup ul {
    list-style: disc;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }

  .writeup li {
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }

  .writeup li::marker {
    color: var(--sec);
  }

  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin: 1.25rem 0;
  }

  .writeup table {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 560px;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .writeup th,
  .writeup td {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--white-icon-tr);
    padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem;
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  .writeup th {
    color: var(--white);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .writeup td {
    color: var(--white-icon);
  }

  .writeup td code {
    word-break: normal;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .transform {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .transform__side {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    min-width: 9.5rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--white-icon-tr);
    border-radius: 1rem;
    background: #1414149c;
  }

  .transform__char {
    font-size: 3.25rem;
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--white);
  }

  .transform__char--accent {
    color: var(--sec);
  }

  .transform__meta {
    color: var(--white-icon);
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .transform__arrow {
    color: var(--sec);
    font-size: 1.4rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .transform {
      gap: 0.5rem;
    }

    .transform__side {
      flex: 1;
      min-width: 0;
      padding: 0.9rem 0.5rem;
    }

    .transform__char {
      font-size: 2.5rem;
    }

    .transform__meta {
      font-size: 0.6rem;
      letter-spacing: 0;
      text-align: center;
    }
  }

  .callout {
    border: 1px solid var(--white-icon-tr);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--sec);
    background: #1414149c;
    border-radius: 0.75rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }

  .callout__label {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
    color: var(--sec);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .callout p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .callout--note {
    border-left-color: var(--white-icon);
  }

  .callout--note .callout__label {
    color: var(--white);
  }

  .flow {
    display: flex;
    align-items: stretch;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }

  .flow__step {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--white-icon-tr);
    background: #1414149c;
    border-radius: 0.75rem;
    padding: 0.9rem;
  }

  .flow__step span {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
    color: var(--white-icon);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .flow__step strong {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .flow__step code {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    word-break: normal;
  }

  .flow__meta {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--white-icon);
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  .flow__step--accent {
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--sec) 45%, transparent);
  }

  .flow__arrow {
    align-self: center;
    color: var(--sec);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .writeup table {
      min-width: 0;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
    }

    .writeup th,
    .writeup td {
      padding: 0.5rem 0.35rem;
    }

    .writeup th {
      font-size: 0.68rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.03em;
    }

    .writeup td code {
      white-space: normal;
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }

    .flow {
      flex-direction: column;
    }

    .flow__arrow {
      transform: rotate(90deg);
      text-align: center;
    }
  }
</style>
